--- body ---
## Context

R3 promises that the cost of staying current is proportional to what changed,
"never to the size of the library" (`SPEC.md` R3). The current acceptance
layer proves a narrower statement:

- `R3-edit-recomputes-only-what-changed` drives one edit in one fixture and
  accepts exactly one record-stage `done` plus that record's section count at
  the embed stage;
- `R3-identical-resync-recomputes-nothing` rejects downstream `done` work but
  accepts any positive amount of `work.*.resync.noop` verification;
- the red fixture `stub-recomputes-whole-library-on-edit` proves the first
  detector notices reported whole-library embedding at that fixture's one
  size.

None repeats the same fixed-size change against different library sizes. None
bounds verification, scanning, or other maintenance work that is absent from
the `*.done` counters. No elapsed-cost or operation-count observable closes
that escape hatch. A target can therefore pass while doing library-sized work
outside downstream recomputation; the identical-resync check in particular
passes when its positive `resync.noop` count grows with the whole library.

This is an acceptance gap, not a finding about zoteus's implementation.
Ticket 0614 cannot conclude that goal 2 keeps R3 until the gap is closed and
the resulting check is run against the target.

## Master-ticket role

This is the master ticket for the R3 acceptance repair. It owns the coverage
inventory, decomposition, dependency accounting, and final integrated verdict.
It does not itself implement the entire matrix below. Executable work goes into
bounded child tickets, created on demand when that work is ready to start.

The master keeps four work packages distinct:

1. the target-neutral cost observable, including verification/scanning work
   rather than only downstream `done` outcomes;
2. the scaled fixture mechanism and its library-sized-work hostile controls;
3. bounded perturbation families drawn from the change matrix; and
4. real-target integration, committed evidence, and the resulting R3 verdict
   in ticket 0614.

Any numeric decision rule belongs in `SPEC.md` first. Any reinterpretation of
R3, including a carve-out for unavoidable discovery scans, is a ruling in
`DECISIONS.md` first. A child must not settle either locally.

## Decomposition rule

Create a child before implementation, sized to one independently reviewable
instrument or perturbation family. A child names 0649 as its master in its body,
carries only its real `Blocked-by` edges, and owns its code, red controls,
tests, real-target run where applicable, and evidence. Work that can run against
fixtures now is not blocked merely because zoteus cannot yet expose or drive the
same event.

Do not file placeholder children for the whole inventory. Spin them off as the
next work becomes actionable, add them to the table below, and leave uncovered
rows visibly unallocated here. Closely related matrix rows may share one child
only when they use one observable and form one reviewable sign-off unit; the
master must not become their implementation ticket.

## Change matrix

Every row is exercised at more than one untouched-library size. The changed
item, its attachment bytes and its section/passage counts stay identical
between scale points. The expected work may grow with the changed object (for
example, deleting one long attachment costs more than deleting one short
attachment); it must not grow with unrelated items.

### Items and record-derived sources

- add an item, both metadata-only and with body-bearing children;
- edit each indexed record input, including title, abstract, creators, date,
  tags and collection membership, distinguishing fields that affect only the
  record from fields that legitimately change downstream keys;
- delete an item and prove every derived copy disappears without visiting or
  rewriting unrelated items; restore/undelete it as a distinct event;
- add, edit and delete a child note;
- add, edit and delete an annotation, including its locator/page metadata;
- move an item into or out of the indexed perimeter, including a library or
  group being added, removed, or becoming unavailable where the target offers
  those events.

### Attachments and extraction

- add an attachment to an item with none;
- add a same-language sibling that D6 skips, and add a different-language
  sibling that D6 selects;
- delete the selected attachment, forcing the deterministic sibling (if any)
  to take its place; delete a skipped attachment and require no unrelated
  recomputation;
- replace attachment bytes under the same key, and edit attachment metadata
  without changing its bytes; relink or move a linked file, make it
  unavailable, and make it available again;
- reparent an attachment between items and remove/restore the attachment
  object without deleting/restoring its parent item;
- change the detected language or deterministic attachment order so the D6
  choice changes;
- make full text appear, change, disappear, become truncated, or become
  extractable only through the alternate pack/flat route;
- transition among extracted, metadata-only, skipped and quarantined states,
  including retry after the input changes.

### Tool, key and reconciliation changes

- identical-bytes resync after a version signal advances;
- extractor upgrade/re-extract with unchanged bytes;
- segmenter or chunk-geometry key bump;
- embedder identity/dtype change and prefix-stale repair;
- interrupted-work resume, proving already-committed records, extraction,
  chunks and vectors are not bought again;
- retry of a failed stage, bounded to that item and the stages downstream of
  the failure;
- a batch containing several changes, checked against the union of their
  dependency cones rather than against the library size.

The trigger roster in `SPEC.md §5.2.8` (`new`, `edit`, `re-extract`, `resync`,
`key-bump`, `prefix-stale`, `retry`, `delete`) is the minimum coverage index:
each trigger needs a positive arm, a local-red control, and a
library-sized-work red control. Rows above may share an arm only when the
artifact separately reports the work attributable to each event.

Deletion discovery needs explicit treatment. `SPEC.md` currently routes it
through census subtraction because Zotero exposes no local `/deleted`
endpoint. If that necessarily performs library-sized scanning, the harness
must expose the conflict with R3's literal "never to the size of the library"
instead of silently excluding discovery cost. Resolving that conflict or
carving discovery out of R3 is a design ruling: record it in `DECISIONS.md`
first and update `SPEC.md` before encoding a different acceptance rule.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] Every applicable matrix row and every `SPEC.md §5.2.8` trigger is assigned
      to a bounded child, or explicitly ruled out at the owning authority.
- [ ] Every child is closed with its own red controls and evidence; unsupported
      real-target rows report not-run with the missing capability named.
- [ ] The integrated suite exercises each fixed change at more than one library
      size and accounts for verification/scanning cost as well as `*.done`
      recomputation.
- [ ] Existing R3 clean and churn controls remain discriminating after the
      child suites are integrated.
- [ ] The complete suite runs against zoteus; its raw evidence and the resulting
      PASS, FAIL, or honest not-run are recorded and linked from 0614.
- [ ] `make check` and `./tickets/erg check` pass.

